            },
            {
                "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                "word": "naggle",
                "pos": "verb",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "fa8a34d1-63de-4eb1-bbe0-3ce63bdf8ccc",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "tags": [
                            "present",
                            "singular",
                            "third-person"
                        ],
                        "form": "naggles"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "8166b80c-e28c-47ee-b0e8-c34628b807ca",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "present"
                        ],
                        "form": "naggling"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "a48ae6f9-a4d2-4daf-bdbe-d6cab0facc8e",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "naggled"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "5430da0c-48e0-4fee-a070-4c72e0db3caa",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "naggled"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "438a1f49-3797-3f73-b044-a79655c45acc",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To toss the head or cause to toss the head."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6bc0c0a2-9e61-4188-9ddd-163e257ce467",
                                "sense_id": "438a1f49-3797-3f73-b044-a79655c45acc",
                                "text": "On the Pieces last Sunday they made such a rout, i That the scurrilous author would soon be found out, Then they naggled their heads, while their tails flew about."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7f46482-b567-4ed1-8642-a250c714b120",
                                "sense_id": "438a1f49-3797-3f73-b044-a79655c45acc",
                                "text": "To manage children so skilfully that they do not know they are being managed is a great art. A good driver never naggles his horse : he lets the bit prove a guide, but not an irritant."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a3519920-a71f-4689-a932-cda0318db7d8",
                                "sense_id": "438a1f49-3797-3f73-b044-a79655c45acc",
                                "text": "Over his prostrate body we ambled, the ill - tempered little beast naggling at its bit, and doing his utmost to unseat me ."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "57d08d63-91fc-3df0-ba10-ab14a2b51282",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To niggle; to irritate or bother continually or repeatedly."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "981a30b5-be7b-4f38-8364-726cd20cb623",
                                "sense_id": "57d08d63-91fc-3df0-ba10-ab14a2b51282",
                                "text": "Nothing obscene, for goodness sake, but just at the bare threshold of audibility, so that it niggles and naggles and bothers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "814f3aca-5080-4de5-9413-02500366caae",
                                "sense_id": "57d08d63-91fc-3df0-ba10-ab14a2b51282",
                                "text": "Or will the story come first? — the \"some incident observed in the subway\" which naggles away at the author, until he begins to write about it, seeing only then (but perhaps not really clearly, himself, ever at all) the point of what he is saying,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dc79380e-5b2e-4a7c-b92c-06e7db5d0126",
                                "sense_id": "57d08d63-91fc-3df0-ba10-ab14a2b51282",
                                "text": "There is Rhoda who has jumped out of an excellent position with the Prudential into some ridiculous place where they bore holes in strips of metal for little boys to play with —Meccano, is it called?—and poor Vera Potter in a sanatorium, they say both lungs are affected—and I still cannot help thinking about Cassica's cousin...Oh, how it all niggles and naggles ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c4619467-40c2-4687-a629-6e69442ecfef",
                                "sense_id": "57d08d63-91fc-3df0-ba10-ab14a2b51282",
                                "text": "But I knew the doctor's words naggled in the back of his mind."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To haggle; to negotiate argumentatively."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "68e92177-136c-405f-b801-6f7ac5265d64",
                                "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                                "text": "Ye see the wives naggle aboot tripe and sheep heeds , Or washing their greens at a fountain, Where the young Nuns used to be telling their beads, And had nowt but thor sins to be countin';"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "629e68c7-faad-4797-977d-c107900ab5f7",
                                "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                                "text": "Nobody wants to naggle about a few months or a year or two, more or less, but the difficulties of the case are exceedingly complicated."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "57e98923-83d4-438f-8bb6-944648aecc9d",
                                "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                                "text": "Under modern conditions and the extremely high cost of estimating plumbing work, he can not afford to estimate or submit bids which will be shopped or naggled after the award of contract."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "69f8744f-1cca-4c44-962b-c81fb5336dc5",
                                "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                                "text": "And I sent along a note saying please would they send me some cans so's I can get this fish under cover before it spoils and after that I guarantee to sit down and fill out another fish crate of forms for OPA and haggle and naggle for as long as they please."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fa388ae6-eb39-45ae-9855-2bd798e4f673",
                                "sense_id": "c04ee0c9-5deb-3d49-9c75-69de3a4dd4b9",
                                "text": "If a Sacaronducan shows energy and enterprise, and puts money together, or founds a business, or starts a mine, all his neighbours promptly take a grab at the plunder, and the Government naggles for the biggest share."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "41305fa8-6589-3240-8aab-672e905a8bdd",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To nag, carp, or quarrel."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c955755d-5684-41db-9e46-d46f973a65f6",
                                "sense_id": "41305fa8-6589-3240-8aab-672e905a8bdd",
                                "text": "Many instances occur daily in which married people go on naggling at one another—naggle, naggle, until they get into a perfect storm—though both would be heartily ashamed for any third party to know what they were quarrelling about."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a314c334-0c79-4961-bd5d-b2e83854787b",
                                "sense_id": "41305fa8-6589-3240-8aab-672e905a8bdd",
                                "text": "A child should be joyous, but how, if he be continually naggled at, can he be?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d4014b4-6fc8-4276-ac47-dfa25cebed9e",
                                "sense_id": "41305fa8-6589-3240-8aab-672e905a8bdd",
                                "text": "When a husband and wife keep their secrets apart, Not a word to \"my spounse\" about this or on that; When a trifle may banish the pledge of their heart, And he naggles—she naggles—both contradict flat; Though unequaled their love when its first blossoms blew, I wouldn't give much for their quiet—would you?"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f35fc253-3fd6-3d09-9a18-7f00de5626d3",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To harass or scold."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9f1ff507-f571-4bd5-89a3-2bf6318b4b42",
                                "sense_id": "f35fc253-3fd6-3d09-9a18-7f00de5626d3",
                                "text": "I 'll go for my paw if folks naggle me ; an' nobody shall tech me !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5885288b-b58f-4b28-a369-87c4d864e7e7",
                                "sense_id": "f35fc253-3fd6-3d09-9a18-7f00de5626d3",
                                "text": "She naggles thy heart out, maybe. But that's just the wrigglin' a place out for hersen."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c27e8938-3ab1-3cca-919c-139df260a5ba",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To make many minor adjustments to; to fiddle with."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1daa4273-b5fd-40c5-9cbe-6cb96e8b05b7",
                                "sense_id": "c27e8938-3ab1-3cca-919c-139df260a5ba",
                                "text": "You can niggle and naggle at any set of documents and always imply that the set you might have put together would have been ever so much better."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a3fdfc1e-01e0-44b3-b090-d5078b9d78b0",
                                "sense_id": "c27e8938-3ab1-3cca-919c-139df260a5ba",
                                "text": "The Bulletin art critic not only praised her 1894 Country Visitors and still-life studies but stated, in typically misogynist Bulletin style, that the latter showed 'a strength sadly lacking in the naggling method of most of her sister- artists'."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "259b31ff-cee1-34bb-8b1a-9abda076906b",
                        "entry_id": "05c84c72-bd16-3d6c-be8e-08b671a32954",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To claw or dig away."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b0ddcea5-6e37-4a5e-a1ba-93ca0f46177c",
                                "sense_id": "259b31ff-cee1-34bb-8b1a-9abda076906b",
                                "text": "\"Water! —water!\" she murmured as though in prayer to it , kneeling by the hole she had naggled in the ice , dabbling her finger in the icy slush that scarce let the water through."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f232b250-a675-4658-8134-575d01be5700",
                                "sense_id": "259b31ff-cee1-34bb-8b1a-9abda076906b",
                                "text": "The group of girls gathered round her as she began to naggle the long strands off, bit by bit, as close to her head as possible."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
